{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2739 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "d6ab7595c8a0505df71487f20e866d32fc7f0f51fa53ea12795a64ee7f09d743" :edb/db-before-t 2738 :edb/db-after-t 2739 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536051 50 #inst "2026-10-09T13:48:01.846Z" 13194139536051 true] [17592186220611 1104 #edb/ref 17592186220613 13194139536051 true] [17592186220615 1104 #edb/ref 17592186220616 13194139536051 true] [17592186220620 1104 #edb/ref 17592186220623 13194139536051 true] [17592186220717 1104 #edb/ref 17592186220722 13194139536051 true] [17592186220729 1104 #edb/ref 17592186220732 13194139536051 true] [17592186221368 1020 #edb/ref 17592186381095 13194139536051 true] [17592186221369 1020 #edb/ref 17592186381099 13194139536051 true] [17592186221940 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536051 true] [17592186221940 1100 "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then, $F(s)$ is twice differentiable at every point $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$ exists for $s>a.$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ and\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from {{field-3}}." 13194139536051 true] [17592186221940 1100 "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[MATH: [0, ∞)]$ and its Laplace transform\n$F(s) = L{f(t)}$\nexists for $s > a$. Then, $F(s)$ is twice differentiable at every point $s > a$, and we have\n$F^{″}(s) = L{t^{2} \\cdot f(t)}$.\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n> $L1$: $F(s) = ∫_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}f(t)dt$ exists for $s > a$.\n> $L2$: $F^{″}(s) = \\frac{d^{2}}{ds^{2}}∫_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}f(t)dt$\n> $L3$: $F^{″}(s) = ∫_{0}^{∞}\\frac{∂^{2}}{∂s^{2}}(e^{-st}f(t))dt$\n> $L4$: $\\frac{∂^{2}}{∂s^{2}}(e^{-st}f(t)) = t^{2}e^{-st}f(t)$\n> $L5$: $F^{″}(s) = ∫_{0}^{∞}t^{2}e^{-st}f(t)dt$\n> $L6$: $F^{″}(s) = L{t^{2} \\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nLine $L2$ follows from line $L1$ and\n{{field-1}}.\n\nLine $L3$ follows from $L2$ and {{field-2}}.\n\nLine $L4$ follows from {{field-3}}." 13194139536051 false] [17592186221940 1145 "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and $s>a,$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nWe're asked to prove the smoothness property in the case $n=2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L2}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we know that\n\n$$\nF(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nBy $\\text{the definition of the second derivative with respect to }s$, we get\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}F(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly the statement in line $\\text{L2}.$\n\n- Next we consider line $\\text{L3}.$ From $\\text{L2}$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is of exponential order, the integral converges, and we can\ndifferentiate under the integral sign:\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt.\n$$\n\nTherefore, line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{differentiating under the integral sign}.$\n\n- We now consider line $\\text{L4}.$ The function $f(t)$ does not depend on $s$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))=f(t)\\,\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st})=f(t)\\,\\frac{∂}{∂s}(-t{e}^{-st})=f(t)\\,({t}^{2}{e}^{-st})={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t).\n$$\n\nThus line $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{the chain rule}.$" 13194139536051 true] [17592186221955 1017 #edb/ref 17592186381106 13194139536051 true] [17592186221955 1017 #edb/ref 17592186381112 13194139536051 true] [17592186221955 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536051 true] [17592186221955 1100 "Suppose we wish to calculate the Laplace transform $L{t^{2}e^{4t}}$ using the smoothness property. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.Let $f(t) = e^{4t}$ and $F(s) = L{f(t)}$. By the smoothness property of Laplace transforms, we have\n$-F^{″}(s)$ {{field-1}}" 13194139536051 false] [17592186221955 1100 "Suppose we wish to calculate the Laplace transform $L{{t}^{2}{e}^{4t}}$ using the smoothness property. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.Let $f(t)={e}^{4t}$ and $F(s)=L{f(t)}.$ By the smoothness property of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}f(t)}={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}{e}^{4t}}={{field-3}},\\,s>4.\n$$" 13194139536051 true] [17592186221955 1145 "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. Moreover, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and every such $s,$ we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{n}f(t)}=(-1)^{n}{F}^{(n)}(s).\n$$\n\nIn particular, for $n=2,$ we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}f(t)}={F}^{″}(s).\n$$\n\nWe have that $f(t)={e}^{4t}.$ Therefore, for $s>4,$ we have\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s-4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{1}{s-4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}((s-4)^{-1}) \\\\  & =-(s-4)^{-2} \\\\ {F}^{″}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-(s-4)^{-2}) \\\\  & =2(s-4)^{-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(s-4)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the smoothness property,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{2}{e}^{4t}}={F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{2}{(s-4)^{3}},\\,s>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536051 true] [17592186221957 1017 #edb/ref 17592186381117 13194139536051 true] [17592186221957 1017 #edb/ref 17592186381122 13194139536051 true] [17592186221957 1017 #edb/ref 17592186381127 13194139536051 true] [17592186221957 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536051 true] [17592186221957 1100 "Suppose we wish to calculate the Laplace transform\n$L{t^{2}\\sin 4t}$.\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below. You may make use of the following result:\n$L{\\sin ωt} = \\frac{ω}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}, s > 0$.Let $f(t) = \\sin 4t$ and $F(s) = L{f(t)}$. By the smoothness property of Laplace transforms, we have\n$F^{″}(s)$ {{field-1}}" 13194139536051 false] [17592186221957 1100 "Suppose we wish to calculate the Laplace transform \n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t}.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below. You may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nLet $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t$ and $F(s)=L{f(t)}.$ By the smoothness property of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}f(t)}={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={{field-2}},\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}{e}^{4t}}={{field-4}},\\,s>0.\n$$" 13194139536051 true] [17592186221957 1145 "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. Moreover, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and every such $s,$ we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{n}f(t)}=(-1)^{n}{F}^{(n)}(s).\n$$\n\nIn particular, for $n=2,$ we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}f(t)}={F}^{″}(s).\n$$\n\nWe have that $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t.$ Using the given result with $\\omega =4,$ the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16},\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(4({s}^{2}+16)^{-1}) \\\\  & =-4({s}^{2}+16)^{-2}\\cdot 2s \\\\  & =-\\frac{8s}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}} \\\\ {F}^{″}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{8s}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-8\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-8\\cdot (\\frac{(s)^{′}\\cdot ({s}^{2}+16)^{2}-s\\cdot {(({s}^{2}+16)^{2})}^{′}}{({s}^{2}+16)^{4}}) \\\\  & =-8\\cdot (\\frac{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}-s\\cdot 2({s}^{2}+16)\\cdot 2s}{({s}^{2}+16)^{4}}) \\\\  & =-8\\cdot (\\frac{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}-4{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+16)}{({s}^{2}+16)^{4}}) \\\\  & =-8\\cdot (\\frac{{s}^{2}+16-4{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+16)^{3}}) \\\\  & =-8\\cdot (\\frac{16-3{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+16)^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{24{s}^{2}-128}{({s}^{2}+16)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the quotient rule to find ${F}^{″}(s).$\n\nTherefore, by the smoothness property,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t}={F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{24{s}^{2}-128}{({s}^{2}+16)^{3}},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536051 true] [17592186381095 1017 #edb/ref 17592186381096 13194139536051 true] [17592186381095 1089 "q-330948" 13194139536051 true] [17592186381095 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536051 true] [17592186381095 1100 "Given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}},$ find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=(3t+4)^{2}$ for $s>0.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536051 true] [17592186381095 1113 #uuid "3c294091-0460-52e0-836a-793055353031" 13194139536051 true] [17592186381095 1145 "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, we expand:\n\n$$\nf(t)=(3t+4)^{2}=9{t}^{2}+24t+16\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity properties, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{9{t}^{2}+24t+16} \\\\  & =L{9{t}^{2}}+L{24t}+L{16} \\\\  & =9L{{t}^{2}}+24L{t}+16L{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.$ Therefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =9L{{t}^{2}}+24L{t}+16L{1} \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+24\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+16\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{24}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{16}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{18+24s+16{s}^{2}}{{s}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(8{s}^{2}+12s+9)}{{s}^{3}},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536051 true] [17592186381096 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381097 13194139536051 true] [17592186381096 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381098 13194139536051 true] [17592186381096 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536051 true] [17592186381096 1041 "field-1" 13194139536051 true] [17592186381096 1104 #edb/ref 17592186381098 13194139536051 true] [17592186381096 1110 #uuid "4673e41b-2429-50a1-b9b4-22206955e8a4" 13194139536051 true] [17592186381097 1034 "\\frac{9}{s^3}+\\frac{24}{s^2}+\\frac{16}{s}" 13194139536051 true] [17592186381097 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536051 true] [17592186381097 1111 #uuid "5a2057fd-fafc-5925-b237-102e4ef70503" 13194139536051 true] [17592186381098 1034 "\\frac{18}{s^3}+\\frac{24}{s^2}+\\frac{16}{s}" 13194139536051 true] [17592186381098 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536051 true] [17592186381098 1111 #uuid "c1a94faa-ef95-51eb-b9b6-a36d71297684" 13194139536051 true] [17592186381099 1017 #edb/ref 17592186381100 13194139536051 true] [17592186381099 1089 "q-332674" 13194139536051 true] [17592186381099 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139536051 true] [17592186381099 1100 "Let $f(t)$ be a piecewise function defined on $[0,\\infty )$ with exponential order $a,$ and consider its Laplace transform \n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)},\\,s>a.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $f(t)$ contains a discontinuity for some value of $t>0,$ we have $F(s)=0$ for all $s>a.$\n- $F(s)$ is infinitely differentiable on $s>a.$\n- The Laplace transform converts a non-smooth $f(t)$ into a smooth function of $s.$" 13194139536051 true] [17592186381099 1113 #uuid "08f4acd9-0162-5e07-b55e-d0e4a1671202" 13194139536051 true] [17592186381099 1145 "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and $s>a,$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Smoothness has no bearing on whether the Laplace transform vanishes.\nA function of exponential order always has a well-defined Laplace transform for $s>a$, and there is no reason for that transform to be identically zero.\n\n- Statement II is true. The smoothness theorem states that for $f(t)$ of exponential order, the Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f}(s)\n$$\n\nis infinitely differentiable at every point in its region of convergence, and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}(s).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The Laplace transform $F(s)$ of a function $f(t